---
/**
 * F/A-22 Raptor - How to Build.
 *
 * An ADDITION to the catalogue, not a replacement of it. The interactive
 * aircraft-scroll sequence and both image layers are ported unchanged from the
 * verified standalone build; only integration edits were applied:
 *   - CSS scoped under .f22build so it cannot leak into the rest of the site
 *   - the standalone's own tokens re-declared on .f22build
 *   - asset paths rewritten to this project's /media locations
 *   - buttons switched to the site's button classes
 *
 * The aircraft is a transparent PNG and the background is a separate PNG.
 * Neither is generated, masked or processed at build time; both are copied
 * byte-for-byte into public/media/aircraft/f-22-raptor/build/.
 */
import Shell from '../../../layouts/Shell.astro';
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE, BASE, pub, abs, bySlug } from '../../../lib/catalog';

const base = BASE;
const ac = '/media/aircraft/f-22-raptor';
const guide = '/media/guides/f-22-raptor';

const a = bySlug.get('f-22-raptor');
if (!a) throw new Error('F-22 Raptor missing from the catalogue');

const crumb = [
  { label: 'Home', href: base },
  { label: 'Catalogue', href: base + 'catalog' },
  { label: a.name, href: `${base}aircraft/${a.slug}` },
  { label: 'How to Build', href: `${base}aircraft/${a.slug}/build/` },
];

const title = 'How to build the ' + a.name;
const description =
  'The ' + a.name + ' park jet build experience: scroll to fly the aircraft into ' +
  'frame, watch it complete a barrel roll, then send it out of frame.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} canonical={abs('/aircraft/f-22-raptor/build/')} image="/og-default.png">
  <Shell>
    <main id="main" class="f22build-page">
      <div class="shell f22build-shell">
        <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
          <ol>
            {crumb.map((c, i) => (
              <li>
                {i < crumb.length - 1 ? (
                  <a href={c.href} data-no-transition>{c.label}</a>
                ) : (
                  <span aria-current="page">{c.label}</span>
                )}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </nav>

<div class="f22build" id="top" data-guide={pub(guide)} data-ac={pub(ac)}>

  <!-- ================= HERO ================= -->
  <div class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Consolidated build guide · 1,610 posts · 108 pages</p>
    <h1>F/A-22 Raptor<small>How to build it</small></h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Steve Shumate's 26-inch foam park jet with working thrust vectoring, consolidated from his
      26-page construction guide and the entire 108-page RCGroups build thread (April 2007 –
      September 2013, 225 builders). The manual tells you what to do. The thread tells you what
      actually goes wrong, and what the designer changed his mind about.
    </p>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn btn--primary" href="#stages">Start the build</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="#watch">Watch the build</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="#community">Community findings</a>
    </div>
    <figure class="hero-fly" id="flight">
      <!-- Aircraft + background are byte-identical copies of the reference
             standalone's own layers: f22-plane-transparent.png is that page's
             plane-cutout.png, f22-background.png is its background.png.
             Neither is re-cut, masked, processed or regenerated. -->
      <div class="hero-fly-sticky" id="flightSticky">
        <img class="hero-fly-bg" src={pub('/media/aircraft/f-22-raptor/build/f22-background.png')} alt="" aria-hidden="true" width="1215" height="864" fetchpriority="high">
        <img class="hero-fly-plane" id="plane" src={pub('/media/aircraft/f-22-raptor/build/f22-plane-transparent.png')} width="1024" height="728"
             alt="F-22 Raptor foam RC park jet in flight, side view, against a blurred green background">
      </div>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <!-- ================= QUICK FACTS ================= -->
  <section id="facts">
    <h2><span class="n">00</span>Quick facts</h2>
    <p class="sub">From the designer's own specification sheet (construction guide p.26, and his opening post).</p>
    <div class="facts">
      <div><dt>Designer</dt><dd>Steve Shumate <span class="mono" style="color:var(--text-faint)">· jetset44</span></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Wingspan</dt><dd>26.0"</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Length</dt><dd>36.3"</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Wing area</dt><dd>248 sq in</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Flying weight</dt><dd>16–18 oz RTF</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Wing loading</dt><dd>10.5 oz/ft²</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Material</dt><dd>6 mm Depron, heat-formed</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Motor</dt><dd>Littlescreamers Park Jet Special</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Battery</dt><dd>Thunder Power 1320 mAh 11.1V Pro-lite</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Propeller</dt><dd>APC 7x4 or 6x5.5 — <strong style="color:var(--orange)">see the prop warning</strong></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Controls</dt><dd>Stabilator, flaperons, rudder (opt), thrust vectoring (opt)</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Current</dt><dd>15 amps nominal · 20 A WOT with 6x4</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Bare airframe</dt><dd>16.7 oz on the prototype</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Fully painted</dt><dd>18.1 oz — the paint costs 1.4 oz</dd></div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <p><strong>Budget the paint, it is not free.</strong> The prototype went from <strong>16.7 oz bare
      to 18.1 oz</strong> finished, with one coat of WBPU/microballoon and two coats of acrylic. The
      designer's rule of thumb is about <strong>1.0 oz for a model this size</strong>, and 1.5–2.0 oz
      for a larger park jet. Every gram you save earlier is what pays for the paint job.</p>
      <p class="src">— jetset44, posts 6846722 &amp; 7998405</p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout warn">
      <p><strong>Your battery may be too small.</strong> The LSPJ draws about <strong>18 A</strong>, but
      the specified Thunder Power 1320 Pro-lite is rated <strong>17 A continuous</strong> — the
      designer's own pack was at its limit. A builder moved to an Apogee 1500 (20C, 30 A): heavier, but
      it matches the motor, and it <em>helped</em> the balance, because the prototype carries roughly
      0.5 oz of nose weight.</p>
      <p class="src">— jetset44, post 7095994, page 26</p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout warn">
      <p><strong>Read this before you plug in a battery.</strong> The manual says 7x4. The designer
      <strong>corrected that in the thread</strong> and told a builder to cut the model back open:</p>
      <p><em>"I did initially recommend an APC 7x4 on this model because that's what worked well on my
      T-38 park jet, but I've since learned that that's way too much prop for this model… On the LSPJ
      you want the max current at WOT to be under 18 amps if at all possible, which is about what my
      Raptor setup draws with a 6x4."</em>
      </p>
      <p class="src">— jetset44, post 7087033, page 25</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= VIDEO ================= -->
  <section id="watch">
    <h2><span class="n">▶</span>See it fly</h2>
    <p class="sub">Videos posted to the build thread. Nothing is loaded from YouTube until you press play.</p>
    <div class="vidpick" role="group" aria-label="Choose a video">
      <button class="is-on" data-vid="dXcSdekd8WA" aria-pressed="true">Scratchbuild</button>
      <button data-vid="2qOs0wHxfx8" aria-pressed="false">Thrust vectoring</button>
      <button data-vid="6iLRDalH1Iw" aria-pressed="false">Maiden flight</button>
    </div>
    <div class="vid" id="vid">
      <button class="vid-f" id="vidBtn" aria-label="Play video: F-22 scratchbuild plane by Alex Ng">
        <img src={pub('/media/aircraft/f-22-raptor/06-1200.webp')} alt="">
        <span class="vid-b">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 68 48" width="64" height="45" aria-hidden="true">
            <path fill="rgba(255,138,61,.28)" d="M66.5 7.7a8.6 8.6 0 0 0-6-6C55.2.2 34 .2 34 .2S12.8.2 7.5 1.7a8.6 8.6 0 0 0-6 6A90 90 0 0 0 0 24a90 90 0 0 0 1.5 16.3 8.6 8.6 0 0 0 6 6C12.8 47.8 34 47.8 34 47.8s21.2 0 26.5-1.5a8.6 8.6 0 0 0 6-6A90 90 0 0 0 68 24a90 90 0 0 0-1.5-16.3Z"/>
            <path fill="#fff" d="M27 34V14l18 10-18 10Z"/>
          </svg>
          Play
        </span>
      </button>
    </div>
    <p class="vidtitle" id="vidTitle">F-22 scratchbuild plane — Alex Ng</p>
    <div class="callout note">
      <p><strong>Why there is no designer walkthrough video.</strong> Steve's own 6mmFlyRC walkthrough
      was the first video in the thread. YouTube has since taken it down, and it can no longer be
      embedded anywhere. The three videos above are the working replacements, all posted by builders
      to the same thread.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= MATERIALS ================= -->
  <section id="materials">
    <h2><span class="n">01</span>Materials &amp; consumables</h2>
    <p class="sub">Everything the guide calls for, grouped by job. Items marked <span class="opt">opt</span> are the designer's suggestions, never requirements. Electrical sizing in the right-hand column is corrected by the thread where the thread supersedes the manual.</p>

    <div class="matgrid mas">
      <div class="mat">
        <h3>Foam</h3>
        <p class="note">Slots on the plans are drawn at 5 mm to suit either 6 mm Depron (tight) or ~5 mm Bluecore (loose).</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>6 mm Depron sheet</b><span>Every part traced from the paper templates. The designer confirms Depron by name when discussing priming.</span></li>
          <li><b>Foam jigs (supplied)</b><span>Hold the vertical tails at the correct dihedral while the epoxy cures.</span></li>
          <li><b>Scrap foam</b><span>Practising bevels and heat forming.</span></li>
          <li><b>Foam sanding pad</b><span>Sanding the spackled fillets.</span></li>
          <li><b>Balsa (opt)</b><span>Several builders substituted 1/2" x 3/8" balsa for the stabilator root, sanded to 6 mm, for a much stronger, slop-free pivot.</span></li>
          <li><b>Foamboard / Bluecore (opt)</b><span>Workable substitute for crash resistance; trim slots undersized to compensate.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="mat">
        <h3>Adhesives</h3>
        <p class="note">The designer's rule: keep epoxy for critical joints, use a light sandable adhesive everywhere else. Weight is the enemy at 16–18 oz.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Epoxy 5-minute</b><span>Motor mount into the fuselage slot; stabilator bearings.</span></li>
          <li><b>Epoxy 30-minute</b><span>Sparing the carbon wing spar into the wing.</span></li>
          <li><b>Microballons</b><span>Mixed into epoxy to cut weight and fill gaps. The single easiest weight saving on the build.</span></li>
          <li><b>Cyanoacrylate thin / thick</b><span>Thin for bearings, horns, end stops. Thick to hold the stabilator servo in its slot.</span></li>
          <li><b>UHU Creativ / UHU POR</b><span>The designer's everyday foam adhesive.</span></li>
          <li><b>ProBond or Gorilla Glue</b><span>Gap-filling structural bonds on the fuselage boxes and the top piece.</span></li>
          <li><b>Aliphatic resin</b><span>Lighter sandable alternative on the aft fuselage top.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="mat">
        <h3>Tapes, fillers &amp; sealants</h3>
        <p class="note">Everything that either protects a hinge or keeps the air out of the foam.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>3M 77 spray</b><span>Every foam lamination, and tacking templates to the sheet.</span></li>
          <li><b>3M Satin tape (purple tub)</b><span>Flaperon and rudder hinges, leading-edge protection. Green-container Scotch is explicitly not good enough.</span></li>
          <li><b>Packing tape</b><span>Heat-forming aid; low-friction wrap on the vectoring stick.</span></li>
          <li><b>Lightweight spackling</b><span>Filling gaps and building fillets. Use the wall-grade version thinned with water for the canopy and nosecone.</span></li>
          <li><b>Water-based polyurethane (WBPU) (opt)</b><span>Seals and stiffens. Microballons in the mix fill holes better.</span></li>
          <li><b>Rubbing alcohol</b><span>Degrease the shell before painting.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="mat">
        <h3>Structure</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Carbon wing spar</b><span>Slotted and epoxied — the primary structure.</span></li>
          <li><b>Carbon tube stabilator pivot</b><span>Runs through two aluminium bearings; the tail boom blocks brace it.</span></li>
          <li><b>Forward bulkheads ×3</b><span>Set the forward fuselage box.</span></li>
          <li><b>Bulkheads F4, F5</b><span>On top of the wing, carrying the aft fuselage sides.</span></li>
          <li><b>Temporary bulkheads ×4</b><span>Hold the aft sides at the right angle, then removed.</span></li>
          <li><b>Turtledeck bulkheads T1–T5</b><span>Support the top piece and show how much heat forming it needs.</span></li>
          <li><b>1/8" plywood side plates ×2</b><span>The vectoring cradle.</span></li>
          <li><b>1/64" plywood scrap</b><span>Bridges the rudder servo hole so the top sits flat on the spine.</span></li>
          <li><b>1/16" rod through the servo shoulder (opt)</b><span>Thread-known reinforcement: a piano-wire pin with a 90° bend, epoxied in, stops the arm ever rotating on the carbon tube.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="mat">
        <h3>Hardware</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Aluminium tube bearing ×1</b><span>In the motor mount stick.</span></li>
          <li><b>Aluminium stabilator bearings ×2</b><span>Pressed into the aft edge of the wing.</span></li>
          <li><b>Spare nylon servo horns</b><span>Drilled out to make the end stops and control arm.</span></li>
          <li><b>Music wire</b><span>Stabilator pushrod.</span></li>
          <li><b>1/16" threaded music wire</b><span>Vectoring pushrod, large Z-bend at the servo end.</span></li>
          <li><b>1/32" music wire (opt)</b><span>Soldered to the rudder cable pushrods.</span></li>
          <li><b>Steel threaded clevis</b><span>Vectoring pushrod to motor mount.</span></li>
          <li><b>Wire — 18 AWG minimum (opt)</b><span>Corrected by the designer: 16 AWG for the long forward run, 18 AWG ESC-to-motor. See the electrical warning.</span></li>
          <li><b>Deans Ultra connectors (opt)</b><span>Micro connectors are only good to ~10 A. The designer's upgrade recommendation.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="mat">
        <h3>Electronics</h3>
        <p class="note">As designed, with the thread's corrections applied.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Littlescreamers Park Jet Special</b><span>The motor the design assumes — its 3/8" stick mount is what the supplied side plates fit.</span></li>
          <li><b>Castle Creations Phoenix 25</b><span>Mounted inside an inlet for airflow. The BEC matters more than most builders expect.</span></li>
          <li><b>Thunder Power 1320 mAh 11.1V Pro-lite</b><span>3S, in the nose.</span></li>
          <li><b>APC 6x4 (corrected)</b><span>Manual says 7x4 or 6x5.5. Thread correction: <strong style="color:var(--orange)">use a 6x4 and stay under 18 A WOT.</strong></span></li>
          <li><b>Futaba S3110 ×4 (or HS-55/HS-65HB)</b><span>Stabilator and flaperons, plus rudder if fitted.</span></li>
          <li><b>Hitec HS-85MG</b><span>Thrust vectoring. Metal geared, 40+ oz/in. A plastic-geared servo strips if the prop hits the ground.</span></li>
          <li><b>Berg 7P (or Berg 4)</b><span>Receiver, installed aft.</span></li>
          <li><b>Watt meter (opt)</b><span>The designer's top recommendation: "one of the best investments you can make in this hobby."</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="mat">
        <h3>Tools</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Sharp hobby knife</b><span>Every bevel, cutout and slot. A long knife or saw for the nosecone.</span></li>
          <li><b>Heat gun</b><span>Clamp it in a bench vice to free both hands. A hair dryer is enough but slow.</span></li>
          <li><b>Drill press</b><span>So the vectoring pivot hole is exactly perpendicular.</span></li>
          <li><b>Wood dowel</b><span>The former for the aft fuselage top compound curves.</span></li>
          <li><b>Sanding block + long bar</b><span>Flattens the lower forward fuselage; squares the bottom edges.</span></li>
          <li><b>Sandpaper 100 / 220 / 320</b><span>100 rough out, 220 shape, 320 polish.</span></li>
          <li><b>Bevel cutter (opt)</b><span>One builder made a jig that cut every bevel on the model in about two minutes.</span></li>
          <li><b>Straightedge</b><span>Straight runs only — the curves are cut freehand.</span></li>
          <li><b>Airbrush + windshield wiper fluid</b><span>Thin acrylic 50/50 with wiper fluid, not water; it dries faster and reduces runs.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout warn">
      <p><strong>The electrical trap nobody warns you about.</strong> A builder used 22 AWG wire and
      Deans micro connectors to save weight. The motor cut out after two seconds at full throttle.
      The fix, and the designer's diagnosis: 22 AWG is a fire risk, 18 AWG is the absolute minimum,
      16 AWG is better, and Deans micro connectors are only rated to about 10 A.</p>
      <p><em>"Lesson: Don't save weight with wire and connectors."</em> — boa279, post 7084581, page 24</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= STAGES ================= -->
  <section id="stages">
    <h2><span class="n">02</span>Build stages</h2>
    <p class="sub">The manual's 26 illustrated pages, grouped into 13 stages in the order the work
    actually happens. Open any page full size to read the dimensions printed on it.</p>
    <div id="stageList"></div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= COMMUNITY ================= -->
  <section id="community">
    <h2><span class="n">03</span>What 1,610 posts taught the builders</h2>
    <p class="sub">Findings that are not in the manual — hard-won, repeated, and in several cases a
    correction to it. Every item is traceable to a post.</p>

    <div class="matgrid mas">
      <div class="mat">
        <h3>Before you commit</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Read the whole guide first</b><span>A builder "corrected" the intentional nose droop with a heat gun before reading the note explaining it. Trim the sides instead.</span></li>
          <li><b>Keep extra foam on hand</b><span>"You have to be prepared to remake parts, so buy enough depron." — first-time builder, page 55.</span></li>
          <li><b>It is not a first park jet</b><span>Designer's own words: "this is NOT the model to start with!" Experienced builders rate it mid-range, but it is more heat forming than his F-14.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="mat">
        <h3>Bevels &amp; forming</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Upper and lower bevels are parallel</b><span>Cut both sides the same way, not mirrored. The gap on the templates is what tells you which edge to cut.</span></li>
          <li><b>Glue first, then heat</b><span>Better method found by a builder: tape the bulkheaded sides together, let them twist, then heat. They hold the angle with the tape off.</span></li>
          <li><b>Don't obsess over bevel accuracy</b><span>"None of the bevels on this ship are really critical, and can be sanded flat with a sanding bar once the model is assembled." — jetset44</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="mat">
        <h3>Weak points — where builders break it</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>The nose section</b><span>The weakest area. With the battery forward as CG demands, any impact loads it. Add fibreglass from ~1" behind the intake forward. One builder found stress cracks at the join <em>just behind the front air intake</em> after high-G flying, and fixed it with flat carbon strips plus two layers of lightweight tissue and two coats of Eazicoat.</span></li>
          <li><b>The vectoring hardwood plates</b><span>Both cracked on one build; replaced in aluminium. Also a known servo-arm/carbon-tube failure point — and the reason not to over-power the model.</span></li>
          <li><b>Vertical tails and wingtips</b><span>Next most fragile. The stabilators are the first thing to go in a high-speed reversal.</span></li>
          <li><b>Carbon tube to servo arm</b><span>Two documented fixes: castellate the arm around the tube, or drill a 1/16" hole through the servo shoulder and the tube and pin it with piano wire.</span></li>
          <li><b>Stones on the field</b><span>One builder fitted a plastic underbody protector cut from acetate tube, because his field is covered in stones after heavy rain. Cheap insurance for a foam belly.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="mat">
        <h3>Launching — the torque roll</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Firm overhand toss, 10–20° nose up</b><span>Steve's own words: a firm, flat toss "helps ensure you have enough airspeed from the get-go to avoid torque rolls." Throwing a pusher jet firm and straight at once takes practice. The thing to avoid is a weak throw, which staggers the model immediately after release.</span></li>
          <li><b>Add a touch of left roll</b><span>Naturally counters the right roll caused by torque.</span></li>
          <li><b>Underhand does not suit this model</b><span>Several builders prefer underhand, but Steve points out the F/A-22 has no flat turtledeck to grab. This is a shape problem, not a technique one.</span></li>
          <li><b>Discus-style works, with a warning</b><span>Grip one wing and throw side-arm. Grip the <strong>carbon spar</strong>, not the skin — "otherwise you'll fail the wing." And never maiden a plane with this throw; it is for after it is well trimmed.</span></li>
          <li><b>Check the thrust line first</b><span>Hold it at the nose, spool up: if it wants to pitch or yaw, fix that before you ever fly it.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="mat">
        <h3>Flying it</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Expo sign is radio-specific</b><span>On a Spektrum, setting "−40% expo" makes it <em>more</em> sensitive, not less. A 20-year veteran lost a plane to exactly this.</span></li>
          <li><b>Back the throttle off slowly at high alpha</b><span>Cutting throttle at high alpha is like a shot of down elevator because of the vectoring.</span></li>
          <li><b>Lose a lot of altitude if you roll off at high alpha</b><span>Not a tip stall, but be ready for rapid sink when banking on final.</span></li>
          <li><b>Rudder needs aileron mixing</b><span>It induces more roll than yaw. Several builders regret fitting rudders on a non-vectoring setup.</span></li>
          <li><b>Flap coupling vs spoilerons</b><span>Designer: normal coupling adds lift (lower landing speed, tighter turns); reverse coupling adds pitch rate (high-alpha control). Try both.</span></li>
          <li><b>Radio needs one programmable mix</b><span>Mix the TV channel to the elevator. A single TV servo can drive both if the bank is thin — but you lose the switch.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="mat">
        <h3>Paint &amp; finishing traps</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Never run WBPU through an airbrush</b><span>Someone tried it and it clogged the airbrush badly. It is a primer, not a spray product — brush it on and sand it off.</span></li>
          <li><b>Do not thin WBPU</b><span>The container says not to, and the designer confirms it. Mix in baby powder until you get the consistency you want; there is no ratio. Microballons fill holes even better.</span></li>
          <li><b>Colour it so you can see it</b><span>Add a little craft paint to the WBPU/baby powder mix. Otherwise the white mixture is nearly invisible on white Depron and you will miss patches.</span></li>
          <li><b>Thin acrylic with wiper fluid, not water</b><span>About 50/50. It dries faster and reduces runs. Two light coats is plenty.</span></li>
          <li><b>Clean the airbrush immediately</b><span>Spray a jar of clean water through the brush and pickup tube when you finish. Even then, paint hides around the nozzle and on the needle — take it apart and wipe those with a paper towel. Under a minute.</span></li>
          <li><b>Clear canopy transforms it</b><span>Multiple builders single out the clear canopy as the single biggest cosmetic improvement to the finished model.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="mat">
        <h3>Variations people actually built</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>120–130% on 6 mm</b><span>Works fine even though the foam isn't thicker. Add a 3 mm CF flat under the stabilators at 90° to the tube — they snapped in a dive without it.</span></li>
          <li><b>Scaling up from 6 mm</b><span>Scale single-sheet to 135% and step to 9 mm foam rather than scaling 6 mm, or the slots and bevels all need manual rework.</span></li>
          <li><b>150% and 180%</b><span>Built and flown by several members, including twin-fan EDF conversions and a 48" span gas-free version.</span></li>
          <li><b>Glasscloth + WBPU (opt)</b><span>Light coat of poly, drape the cloth, second coat, sand 300–400, then baby powder in poly to fill the weave. Roughly +100 g on one build, considered worth it.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
