tools:['Packing tape','Heat gun','Wood dowel & bench vice','Spackling','Foam sanding pad','Pins & tape','Berg 7P','Phoenix 25 ESC'],
tip:'Heat gun in a bench vice, dowel on your chest, roll the piece across it — practise on scrap first. Cooling matters more than it looks: the model needs five servos while most ESCs are rated for three or four, so airflow over the integrated BEC is what lets it run them all safely.',
check:['Top piece heat formed without wrinkles','Gaps spackled and sanded','Electronics as far forward as possible','ESC in moving air, shrink wrap opened','All controls tested before the bottom goes on']},
{n:'12',t:'Inlets, bottom & finishing',p:[19,20,21,22,23],intro:'Diverter assemblies, the bottom, shaping and sanding, launch fairings, then paint.',what:[
'Laminate the inlet diverter to the inlet side piece with 3M 77. Make two assemblies and glue them to the inlet fronts.',
'Cut the bottom piece bevels and the exhaust nozzle bevels, and carve a notch between the nozzles for pushrod and clevis clearance.',
'Sand across the bottom edges with a long bar until flat and straight, trim to fit, then glue and tape.',
'Glue on the forward fuselage bottom piece.',
'Cut slots for the diverters to exit, trim with sandpaper, and slope the inlet leading edges if you want the scale look.',
'Final sand the whole model. Optional: sand the launch fairings to a feathered shape and glue them in at the CG.',
'Degrease with rubbing alcohol, fill the canopy and nosecone with thinned wall spackling, then WBPU and paint.'],
tools:['3M 77','Diverter & side pieces','Long sanding bar','Gap-filling glue','Hobby knife','Sandpaper','Launch fairings (opt)','Rubbing alcohol','Wall spackling','WBPU + baby powder (opt)','Acrylic craft paint','Airbrush','Wiper fluid'],
tip:'The wide fuselage with angled sides is hard to grip for a hand launch — the small side fairings resemble the real aircraft\'s landing gear blisters but sit lower so your fingers rest behind them. For paint, use water-based acrylic craft paint thinned about <strong>50/50 with windshield wiper fluid</strong>, not water: it dries faster and reduces runs. Two light coats is enough. The designer uses Delta Ceramcoat because it is smooth and will not clog an airbrush, and a Badger Crescendo with the medium nozzle, which handles both base coats and camouflage. Expect about 1.4 oz total including the WBPU coat. Always set the airbrush down over a jar of clean water and spray it through afterwards, and take it apart to wipe paint off the nozzle and needle.',
check:['Nozzle notch clears pushrod and clevis','Bottom flat and straight along its length','Diverter exit slots open','Fairings, if fitted, at the CG tick','Whole model finally sanded','Airbrush cleaned straight after painting']},
{n:'13',t:'Setup & first flight',p:[25,26],intro:'The designer\'s own flight setup. All figures are from his specification sheet.',what:[
'Find the CG on the <strong>assembly drawing</strong>, not the parts templates — it is only shown there, so download that file before you start cutting.',
'Set the stabilators slightly leading-edge down at neutral. This is deliberate and gives natural positive static stability.',
'Stabilators ±1.5" at −40% expo. Ailerons ±1.0" at −40% expo. Rudder ±0.5" at −20% expo.',
'Thrust vectoring ±15° mixed with the elevator.',
'Mix the vectoring servo linearly with the elevator: full aft stick should give 1.5" of stabilator AND 15° of up vectoring. The designer needed a 50% rate; put it on a switch if you can.',
'Start with the CG at 3.0" behind the wing root leading edge. It is already conservative — a beginner can go 1/8" further forward for more stability. Ballast in the nose if needed.',
'Hand launch near the CG at about 75% throttle, moderately hard, straight ahead and slightly nose up. Keep your hand clear of the prop.',
'Bring the throttle up slowly once it has speed and a little altitude.',
'On landing, release the elevator just before touchdown and pull the throttle to zero.'],
tools:['Transmitter with a programmable mix','Receiver','Battery','Ballast if needed'],
tip:'Start at 15° of vectoring — plenty of manoeuvrability without feeling pitchy. 30° is possible but needs a switch. Never launch at full throttle: prop torque will roll it before there is airspeed to counter it. The model is otherwise docile, with no bad habits. <strong>Know what the vectoring is doing for you:</strong> turn it off and the stabilators alone will not hold the model up. "You would have to move the CG forward quite a bit (about 10–15% MAC) for the Raptor to become statically stable without the horizontal stabilizers." It flies well partly because the CG is already very far forward, and partly because the tail is doing less work than you would think — only the very tip of the root chord meets the wing, so the outboard 80% of the stabilator span behaves like a conventional stabilizer.',
check:['Deflections measured at the root trailing edge','Stabilators set slightly leading-edge down at neutral','Mix gives 1.5" and 15° at full aft stick','CG at 3.0" behind the root leading edge','Vectoring servo free through its full range']}
